/* book Worked example: Q: An nMOS has W = 1 µm, L = 0.25 µm, Cox = 8.6 fF/µm², µn = 500 cm²/V·s, vsat = 10⁷ cm/s and λ = 0.1 V⁻¹, biased at Vov = 2 V. Find (a) the square-law saturation current, (b) Ec, EcL and VDSsat, (c) the velocity-saturated current and the ratio of the two, (d) ro at that current.
   1. SI units: µn = 0.05 m²/V·s, Cox = 8.6 × 10⁻³ F/m², vsat = 10⁵ m/s, W/L = 4.
   2. (a) kn′(W/L) = 0.05 × 8.6 × 10⁻³ × 4 = 1.72 × 10⁻³ A/V²; ID = ½ × 1.72 × 10⁻³ × 2² = 3.44 mA.
   3. (b) Ec = vsat/µn = 10⁵/0.05 = 2 × 10⁶ V/m; EcL = 2 × 10⁶ × 0.25 × 10⁻⁶ = 0.5 V.
   4. VDSsat = 0.5 × [√(1 + 2 × 2/0.5) − 1] = 0.5 × (√9 − 1) = 1.0 V, half the square-law value of 2 V.
   5. (c) IDsat = 10⁻⁶ × 8.6 × 10⁻³ × 10⁵ × (2 − 1.0) = 0.86 mA. Ratio = 3.44/0.86 = 4.0.
   6. Check by the linear-region formula at VDS = 1.0 V: 1.72 × 10⁻³ × (2 × 1 − 1²/2)/(1 + 1/0.5) = 1.72 × 10⁻³ × 1.5/3 = 0.86 mA. ✓
   7. (d) ro = 1/(0.1 × 0.86 × 10⁻³) = 11.6 kΩ.
   Answer: Square law 3.44 mA; velocity saturation gives 0.86 mA at VDSsat = 1.0 V, four times less; ro ≈ 11.6 kΩ. (With L = 2 µm the same formula gives VDSsat = 1.66 V, close to the square-law 2 V.) */
