/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Gate and channel form a parallel-plate capacitor through the oxide: Cox = εox/tox per unit area, and the gate area gives Cox·W·L.
   - The source and drain diffusions spread a distance Lov under the gate edges, so the gate also overlaps them.
   - Cov = Cox·W·Lov, stored in SPICE as CGSO and CGDO per unit width (Cov = CGSO·W)
   - Overlap capacitance is there in every region.
   - The intrinsic part, Cox·W·L, connects the gate to whichever conductor sits under it.
   - In cutoff there is no channel, so the gate sees the body through the oxide.
   - Cgd matters more than its size suggests: in an inverter the drain swings opposite to the gate, so Cgd is charged by both swings (the Miller effect) and counts about twice in delay estimates.
   - Source and drain form reverse-biased pn junctions with the body, giving Csb and Cdb.
   - Cj = Cj0 / (1 + VR/φB)m
   - Cj0 is the zero-bias capacitance, φB the built-in potential (about 0.7 to 0.9 V) and m the grading coefficient (about 0.5 for the bottom, 0.33 for the sidewall).
*/
